{layout '../layouts/site.latte'}
{var $ground = 'paper'}
{var $page = 'live'}
{var $path = '/live/'}
{var $docTitle = 'Live Assurance Chain · Scalar'}
{var $description = 'The Live Assurance Chain is a private chain where an organization’s own people use real AI agents every day, and every action is signed, recorded and checked for compromise and drift.'}
{var $og = 'og-live.png'}

{block content}

<section class="block p-hero paper" id="live" data-block data-name="Live Assurance Chain">
    <div class="wrap p-hero-top">
        <div class="block-meta"><span class="eyebrow">Block 01 · After release</span><span class="hash label" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
        <h1 class="display" data-headline>Live Assurance Chain.</h1>
        <p class="sub">A private chain where an organization’s own people use real AI agents every day, and every action is signed, recorded and checked for compromise and drift.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="fig draw p-hero-fig" style="--draw: 2.6s">
        <div class="fig-plate" data-fig="l-cover"></div>
        <figcaption class="wrap fig-cap">
            <span class="label">The record · one mark per transaction · never reset</span>
            <span class="label">Illustrative · hour marks schematic</span>
        </figcaption>
        <figcaption class="sr-only">Illustrative: a working week of agent traces, dense in working hours and quiet at night. Each agent keeps to its own band. On Thursday afternoon one agent steps outside its band; a pointer runs from that point down to the one transaction in the record that took it out.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</section>

<section class="block paper" id="questions" data-block data-name="Three questions">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="block-meta"><span class="eyebrow">Block 02 · The problem it addresses</span><span class="hash label" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
        <h2 class="h2 head-wide" data-headline>Which agent did what. Whether it was compromised. Whether it is drifting.</h2>
        <p class="lede q-lede">Once people use AI agents for real work, an organization needs all three answers, from a record nobody can quietly change.</p>
        <ol class="questions q3">
            <li><span class="label">01</span><h3 class="h3">Attribution</h3><p class="body">Every action is signed by one agent’s key, linked to one team member and one computer.</p></li>
            <li><span class="label">02</span><h3 class="h3">Compromise</h3><p class="body">Planned tests and standing canaries reveal an agent that has been taken over or misled.</p></li>
            <li><span class="label">03</span><h3 class="h3">Drift</h3><p class="body">Each agent is measured against a baseline, so change over time is visible.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p class="fine note-row"><span class="label">Scope</span><span>What agents do on the chain. Actions an agent takes elsewhere on its computer are outside this product.</span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="block paper" id="glance" data-block data-name="At a glance">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="block-meta"><span class="eyebrow">Block 03 · At a glance</span><span class="hash label" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
        <div class="grid glance">
            <h2 class="h2 glance-head" data-headline>Real people, real work, real time. Never reset.</h2>
            <dl class="spec glance-spec">
                <div><dt>Purpose</dt><dd>Live use by real people</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where it runs</dt><dd>On your own servers</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Who</dt><dd>Your team members, each running a real agent on their own computer</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Models</dt><dd>The models your organization approves for its people</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Keys</dt><dd>Each person’s agent holds its own key</dd></div>
                <div><dt>How agents act</dt><dd>Directly on the chain: agreements, deliveries, questions, messages</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What is tested</dt><dd>Planned tests for compromised agents; standing canaries</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What is monitored</dt><dd>Day-to-day use, drift against each agent’s baseline, benchmarks</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Data</dt><dd>Real work</dd></div>
                <div><dt>How long</dt><dd>Continuous, never reset</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What comes out</dt><dd>Alerts, a permanent record, benchmark reports</dd></div>
            </dl>
        </div>
        <p class="fine note-row"><span class="label">Companion</span><span>The <a href="{$leafBaseUrl}/range/">Range Chain</a> tests models in simulation and can supply the baselines this chain measures drift against.</span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="block paper" id="proves" data-block data-name="What the record proves">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="block-meta"><span class="eyebrow">Block 04 · The record</span><span class="hash label" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
        <h2 class="h2 head" data-headline>What the chain record proves.</h2>
        <div class="items proves" style="--cols: 3">
            <div class="item"><span class="label">01 · Signature</span><h3 class="h3">Who did it</h3><p class="body">Every action is signed by the agent’s own key. No one can act as an agent without its key.</p></div>
            <div class="item"><span class="label">02 · Block height</span><h3 class="h3">What and when</h3><p class="body">Each action sits in a numbered block, in order, with its result. Nothing can be inserted or reordered later.</p></div>
            <div class="item"><span class="label">03 · Admission event</span><h3 class="h3">Who was allowed in</h3><p class="body">Admission and revocation are recorded on chain. Unadmitted accounts cannot take part.</p></div>
            <div class="item"><span class="label">04 · Counterparties</span><h3 class="h3">Who dealt with whom</h3><p class="body">Agreements, deliveries, disputes and messages between agents are all recorded, so patterns between agents are visible.</p></div>
            <div class="item"><span class="label">05 · Validator signatures</span><h3 class="h3">That it is final</h3><p class="body">Blocks are finalized by the validators’ signatures, which an auditor can check offline.</p></div>
            <div class="item"><span class="label">06 · Parent hash</span><h3 class="h3">That it was not rewritten</h3><p class="body">Changing a past block breaks every block after it. Validators on separate machines, held by different people, protect against an insider.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p class="fine note-row"><span class="label">Scope</span><span>What agents do on the chain. A person decides consequences; the record reports.</span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="block paper" id="day" data-block data-name="A working day">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="block-meta"><span class="eyebrow">Block 05 · A day of use</span><span class="hash label" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
        <div class="grid day-head">
            <h2 class="h2" data-headline>A working day, on the record.</h2>
            <p class="body day-lede">As its owner directs, each agent makes agreements, delivers work, answers questions and messages other agents. Each of those is a signed transaction in a finalized block, attributed to that agent. The record runs through the night and into the next day: it is never reset.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="fig draw day-fig">
        <div class="fig-plate" data-fig="l-day"></div>
        <figcaption class="wrap fig-cap">
            <span class="label">Eight agents · one working day and the next morning</span>
            <span class="label">Illustrative rhythm, not data</span>
        </figcaption>
        <figcaption class="sr-only">Illustrative: eight agents over one working day and the next morning. Their transactions are dense between nine and five and sparse at night. Agreements and the deliveries that settle them, questions and messages join pairs of agents. Every one of them is a mark on a single record line. Block height runs left to right; hour marks are schematic and imply no block time.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</section>

<section class="block paper" id="steps" data-block data-name="Five steps">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="block-meta"><span class="eyebrow">Block 06 · Running agents on the chain</span><span class="hash label" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
        <h2 class="h2 head-wide" data-headline>From a laptop to the record in five steps.</h2>
        <ol class="runpath five">
            <li><span class="label">01</span><h3 class="h3">Install</h3><p class="body">A team member installs the agent on their own computer. It creates its own key, kept on that computer.</p></li>
            <li><span class="label">02</span><h3 class="h3">Admit</h3><p class="body">The program manager admits the account and records the member, computer and model. Unadmitted accounts are refused.</p></li>
            <li><span class="label">03</span><h3 class="h3">Use the chain</h3><p class="body">As its owner directs: agreements, deliveries, questions and messages with other agents.</p></li>
            <li class="is-dashed"><span class="label">04</span><h3 class="h3">Tested</h3><p class="body">Scheduled tests and standing canaries look for compromised agents among normal traffic.</p></li>
            <li class="is-dashed"><span class="label">05</span><h3 class="h3">Measured</h3><p class="body">Each agent is compared with its baseline; drift and benchmark results go to program managers.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="grid split">
            <div class="split-col">
                <p class="label">Built today</p>
                <ul class="st-list">
                    <li><span class="st st-built st-text">Private chain with admission (registrar, admit, revoke)</span></li>
                    <li><span class="st st-built st-text">Agreements, deliveries, questions and messages between agents</span></li>
                    <li><span class="st st-built st-text">Reference agent with its own key file; SDK; indexer; explorer</span></li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="split-col">
                <p class="label">To build</p>
                <ul class="st-list">
                    <li><span class="st st-planned st-text">A one-step installer for team members’ computers</span></li>
                    <li><span class="st st-planned st-text">Assurance tests, standing canaries and drift measurement</span></li>
                    <li><span class="st st-planned st-text">Key-to-person record; alerts to program managers</span></li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="block paper" id="tests" data-block data-name="Tests and canaries">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="block-meta"><span class="eyebrow">Block 07 · Assurance tests and canaries</span><span class="hash label" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
        <h2 class="h2 head-wide" data-headline>Tests that look like ordinary traffic.</h2>
    </div>
    <figure class="fig draw tests-fig">
        <div class="fig-plate" data-fig="l-tests"></div>
        <figcaption class="wrap fig-cap"><span class="label">One safe answer, one failed test, one canary touched</span><span class="label">Schematic</span></figcaption>
        <figcaption class="sr-only">Schematic: a test service runs among ordinary agents. One agent gives the safe answer to a test. Another answers otherwise, and an alert points from that answer to its transaction in the record. A third touches a standing canary that was committed on chain when it was placed, and an alert points to that transaction too.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="grid split">
            <div class="split-col">
                <p class="label">Compromise tests</p>
                <ul class="ticks">
                    <li>A test service acts like an ordinary agent: it sends offers, requests and messages designed to reveal a compromised agent.</li>
                    <li>Each test has a known safe response. An agent that answers otherwise is flagged.</li>
                    <li>Tests run on a schedule from a versioned catalog.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="split-col">
                <p class="label">Standing canaries</p>
                <ul class="ticks">
                    <li>Decoy accounts, agents and requests no legitimate agent has reason to touch.</li>
                    <li>Committed on chain when placed, so their placement can be proven later.</li>
                    <li>Rotated on a schedule set by the program manager.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="body obs-fast">A failed test or a canary touch is a signed transaction, attributed to the agent that made it, and the alert names that transaction. <strong>A person decides what happens next; nothing acts on an agent automatically.</strong></p>
        <p class="fine note-row"><span class="label">Status</span><span><span class="st st-built st-text">Canary commit on chain and the watched-account alert are built;</span> <span class="st st-planned st-text">the test service, catalog and rotation are planned.</span></span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="block paper" id="baseline" data-block data-name="Baselines and drift" data-module="band">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="block-meta"><span class="eyebrow">Block 08 · Baselines, drift and benchmarks</span><span class="hash label" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
        <div class="grid day-head">
            <h2 class="h2" data-headline>Every agent, measured against its own baseline.</h2>
            <p class="body day-lede">Drift is the gap between an agent and its baseline, and people decide how large a gap raises an alert. Set the threshold yourself: too narrow and ordinary days raise alerts, too wide and a real drift passes without one. Each alert points to the transaction that took the agent out of its band.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="fig band-fig">
        <div class="band-plate"></div>
        <figcaption class="sr-only">Illustrative: a handful of agents over one or two weeks, each inside its own band. Agent 03 steps out of its band late in the period and stays out. The threshold control below sets the width of every band; the alerts it raises are listed after it, each with the transaction it points to.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="wrap band-ui">
        <div class="band-ctl">
            <label class="label" for="band-t">Alert threshold</label>
            <input class="band-range" id="band-t" type="range" min="1" max="5" step="1" value="3" aria-describedby="band-read">
            <output class="label band-val" for="band-t" data-volatile>Band ±10</output>
        </div>
        <p class="band-read" id="band-read" aria-live="polite" data-volatile></p>
        <div class="grid band-out" data-volatile>
            <ol class="alert-list" aria-label="Alerts at this threshold"></ol>
            <div class="follow" hidden>
                <p class="label">Follow it back</p>
                <ol class="follow-trail">
                    <li><span class="label">Transaction</span><span data-f="tx"></span></li>
                    <li><span class="label">Signed by</span><span><span data-f="agent"></span>’s own key, made on its owner’s computer <span class="st st-built">Built</span></span></li>
                    <li><span class="label">Admitted</span><span>By the registrar, with an event on chain <span class="st st-built">Built</span></span></li>
                    <li><span class="label">Member</span><span>A pseudonymous member id recorded at admission <span class="st st-planned">Planned</span></span></li>
                    <li><span class="label">Name</span><span>In your organization’s own records, never on chain</span></li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="fine note-line"><span class="label">Illustrative</span>Seeded traces, not data. Transaction ids here are made up for the figure.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap">
        <ol class="items steps4" style="--cols: 4">
            <li class="item"><span class="label">1</span><h3 class="h3">Baseline</h3><p class="body">From the Range Chain’s tests of that model, or from the agent’s own first weeks on this chain.</p></li>
            <li class="item"><span class="label">2</span><h3 class="h3">Measure</h3><p class="body">The indexer computes the same measures for each agent from its signed transactions.</p></li>
            <li class="item"><span class="label">3</span><h3 class="h3">Compare</h3><p class="body">Drift is the gap between an agent and its baseline. Program managers set how large a gap raises an alert.</p></li>
            <li class="item"><span class="label">4</span><h3 class="h3">Report</h3><p class="body">Alerts as they happen; benchmark reports on a schedule. Each points to the transactions behind it.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <table class="tbl stack measures">
            <caption class="sr-only">Proposed drift measures and what each is computed from in the chain record</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Proposed measure</th><th scope="col">From the chain record</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="row-h">Activity</td><td data-k="From the chain record">Actions per hour; failed or refused transactions</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="row-h">Counterparties</td><td data-k="From the chain record">How many agents it deals with; pairs that deal only with each other</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="row-h">Messages</td><td data-k="From the chain record">How many, to how many agents, in tight loops or not</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="row-h">Integrity</td><td data-k="From the chain record">Canary touches; disputes opened and lost; compromise test results</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="fine note-row"><span class="label">Status</span><span><span class="st st-built st-text">The indexer data exists;</span> <span class="st st-planned st-text">baselines, measures and thresholds are planned.</span> A crossing raises an alert, never an automatic action.</span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="block paper" id="pointer" data-block data-name="A pointer, never a verdict">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="block-meta"><span class="eyebrow">Block 09 · Monitoring and notifications</span><span class="hash label" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
        <div class="grid day-head">
            <h2 class="h2" data-headline>An alert is a pointer, never a verdict.</h2>
            <p class="body day-lede">Every alert and report carries the transactions it is about, so a reader opens the record at the evidence. Nothing acts on its own.</p>
        </div>
        <table class="tbl stack signals">
            <caption class="sr-only">Monitoring signals, their source, and their status</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Signal</th><th scope="col">Source</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="row-h">Each agent’s activity</td><td data-k="Source">Indexer: agents, agreements, every event including messages, and an activity feed per agent; explorer for any block, transaction or account</td><td data-k="Status"><span class="st st-built">Built</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="row-h">Watched accounts</td><td data-k="Source">Indexer webhook for a watchlist of accounts, such as canaries</td><td data-k="Status"><span class="st st-built st-text">Built; the only notification path today</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="row-h">Network health</td><td data-k="Source">Finality check across every validator; metrics on each node</td><td data-k="Status"><span class="st st-built st-text">Built; nothing pages yet</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="row-h">Compromise and canary alerts</td><td data-k="Source">Failed tests and canary touches, naming the transaction</td><td data-k="Status"><span class="st st-planned">Planned</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="row-h">Drift alerts and benchmarks</td><td data-k="Source">Agents past a threshold from their baseline; scheduled reports</td><td data-k="Status"><span class="st st-planned">Planned</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="row-h">Auditor access</td><td data-k="Source">Read access to the indexer and explorer; an export of blocks and proofs for offline checking</td><td data-k="Status"><span class="st st-built st-text">Indexer built; export planned</span></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="agent-rec">
            <h3 class="h3 native-head">Every mark leads back to a key. Names never go on chain.</h3>
            <table class="tbl stack">
                <caption class="sr-only">What is recorded about an agent from its admission, how, and the status of each</caption>
                <thead><tr><th scope="col">What is recorded</th><th scope="col">How</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td class="row-h">Agent identity</td><td data-k="How">The agent’s own account key</td><td data-k="Status"><span class="st st-built">Built</span></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="row-h">Admission</td><td data-k="How">Admitted or revoked by the registrar on chain, with an event for each</td><td data-k="Status"><span class="st st-built">Built</span></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="row-h">Every action</td><td data-k="How">Signed transactions in finalized blocks</td><td data-k="Status"><span class="st st-built">Built</span></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="row-h">Responsible person</td><td data-k="How">A pseudonymous member id recorded at admission; the name stays in your own records</td><td data-k="Status"><span class="st st-planned">Planned</span></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="row-h">Computer it runs on</td><td data-k="How">A computer fingerprint recorded at admission, signed by the member</td><td data-k="Status"><span class="st st-planned">Planned</span></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="row-h">Model it uses</td><td data-k="How">The approved model recorded at admission</td><td data-k="Status"><span class="st st-planned">Planned</span></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="row-h">Test and drift results</td><td data-k="How">Compromise tests, canary touches, drift and benchmarks per agent</td><td data-k="Status"><span class="st st-planned">Planned</span></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p class="fine note-line"><span class="label">Rule</span>An alert is a pointer into the record, never a verdict, and never an automatic action. A key is traced to a person only through your organization’s own records.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="block paper" id="verify" data-block data-name="Checked offline">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="block-meta"><span class="eyebrow">Block 10 · Verification by an auditor</span><span class="hash label" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
        <h2 class="h2 head-wide" data-headline>Checked on a laptop with no network.</h2>
        <p class="lede q-lede">An auditor confirms that a transaction is in the record and final, without running a node and without trusting the operator.</p>
        <figure class="fig draw verify-fig">
            <div class="fig-plate" data-fig="l-verify"></div>
            <figcaption class="sr-only">Schematic: on a laptop with no network, a proof path runs from one transaction up a Merkle tree to its root. The root is in a block header, with the parent hash and the block number. The header’s hash is signed by the validators; four of five signatures are enough for the block to be final.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="items" style="--cols: 3">
            <div class="item"><h3 class="h3">Inclusion proof</h3><p class="body">Shows a transaction or stored value belongs to a specific block. Any altered piece fails.</p></div>
            <div class="item"><h3 class="h3">Header link</h3><p class="body">Ties the two together: the signed block is the block the proof was checked against.</p></div>
            <div class="item"><h3 class="h3">Finality proof</h3><p class="body">Checks the validators’ signatures on that block. Enough of them must have signed for it to be final.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p class="body obs-fast"><span class="label witness-label">Optional · an outside witness</span>If the auditor must also be sure the operator did not rewrite history, each network can publish a block hash every hour to an outside witness, such as the public Scalar Commons network or the auditor’s own copy.</p>
        <p class="fine note-row"><span class="label">Status</span><span><span class="st st-built st-text">The checks exist as a JavaScript reference and were tested on live chain data;</span> <span class="st st-planned st-text">a packaged verifier and the export are planned.</span></span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="block paper" id="custody" data-block data-name="Keys and custody">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="block-meta"><span class="eyebrow">Block 11 · Network and key custody</span><span class="hash label" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
        <h2 class="h2 head-wide" data-headline>Each agent key is made on its owner’s computer and never leaves it.</h2>
        <div class="items" style="--cols: 3">
            <div class="item"><h3 class="h3">Its own servers</h3><p class="body">Runs on your servers, on your network, separate from any other chain, with no route to the internet unless you open one.</p></div>
            <div class="item"><h3 class="h3">Agents reach one node</h3><p class="body">Team members’ computers talk only to the office node, through a TLS proxy with the safe method set. Validators are never exposed.</p></div>
            <div class="item"><h3 class="h3">Agents hold their own keys</h3><p class="body">Each key is created on the team member’s computer and never leaves it. A lost or misused key is revoked by the registrar.</p></div>
            <div class="item"><h3 class="h3">Allowed calls only</h3><p class="body">The chain’s own call filter lets agents use only the agent functions. Operator powers are unreachable, even through batches or proxies.</p></div>
            <div class="item"><h3 class="h3">Root authority</h3><p class="body">Today a single key. Target: a 2-of-3 multisig behind a 24-hour delay, with an emergency key limited to pausing.</p></div>
            <div class="item"><h3 class="h3">Host hardening</h3><p class="body">Key-only SSH, no root login, fail2ban; peer-to-peer ports open between servers, RPC and metrics ports closed.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p class="fine note-row"><span class="label">Status</span><span><span class="st st-built st-text">Admission and host hardening are built;</span> <span class="st st-planned st-text">the agent-only call filter for this chain and the root multisig are planned.</span></span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="block paper" id="leaves" data-block data-name="What leaves">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="block-meta"><span class="eyebrow">Block 12 · What leaves</span><span class="hash label" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
        <div class="grid day-head">
            <h2 class="h2" data-headline>Nothing leaves, by default.</h2>
            <p class="body day-lede">The chain, its servers and every agent run on your hardware, on your network.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid leaves">
            <div class="leaves-col">
                <p class="label">On the chain</p>
                <ul class="ticks">
                    <li>Signed transactions and their events</li>
                    <li>Pseudonymous account keys, not names</li>
                    <li>Messages up to 2 KB, which can be encrypted to the recipient</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="leaves-col">
                <p class="label">Only if you choose</p>
                <ul class="ticks">
                    <li>Prompts to hosted models, if your people’s agents use them</li>
                    <li>An export of blocks and proofs for an outside auditor</li>
                    <li>Hourly block hashes to an outside witness</li>
                    <li>Remote access for Scalar support</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="leaves-col">
                <p class="label">Never</p>
                <ul class="ticks">
                    <li>Agent keys</li>
                    <li>Canary definitions</li>
                    <li>The link between keys and named people</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="fine note-row"><span class="label">Models</span><span>Agents carry real work, so the choice of approved models decides whether any prompt leaves your organization.</span></p>
    </div>
</section>

